                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\"Who's Lila?\" is a horror mystery game where you're put into the shoes of the culprit trying to hide the fact you did it by controlling your facial expressions with mouse inputs as if you're playing a DS game with the DS turned sideways. At first it was a novel experience. The first \"run\" intrigued me. Then I'm told to try to get the other endings. Okay. Unfortunately the decisions and paths to get the different endings don't really stray much from the first string of events you see the first time. Almost every run has you arguing with Martha and Graves on the rooftop. Almost every run has you going the same exact path. This wouldn't be a problem if William didn't walk at the speed of an 80 year old with prostate cancer. Our \"hero\" simply has no sense of urgency for someone who is \"suspected\" of murder. \n\nOne of the first things that came to my mind as soon as I opened the game was, \"Wow! This REALLY hurts to look at.\" The description for the game touts a \"dithered art-style\" without even worrying about actual visual clarity. Half the time I had NO idea what I was looking at in-game. I just held SPACE and clicked on the interactables. They could have pulled something really cool off but they just present you with an absolute MESS of pixels more than half the time.\n\nThe biggest gameplay mechanic has you shaping Williams face to present the \"appropriate\" emotion in conversations and interrogation. While this is cool at first. I found myself often clicking the wrong spots to control the face parts. Like the face muscle movement is extremely inconsistent and is detrimental to making progress in game. I'll want to raise the eyebrows but the game will say that I clicked the eyelids. I'll want to open the eyes wider and it will say I clicked the eyebrow ridge. I found this to be extremely frustrating on multiple playthroughs. Especially during the parts where his face will contort against your will good luck even clicking and dragging the part you want. Along with this, saving is extremely limited even though it TELLS you to literally save scum if you reach a wall in a run.\n\nThe soundtrack is okay and \"fits\" the atmosphere of the game but the overall sound design is trash. There are so many parts in the game where your audio will peak for NO REASON. Walk by an electrical pole? Here's a LOUD SOUND. Run into something in the boiler room? Here's a LOUD SOUND. You're literally killing someone? Here's multiple loud sounds to peak your audio! I found this to be more annoying than anything. Even if I lowered the master volume in game I was still turning down the game at some points because they decided to go with the LOUD = SCARY/UNSETTLING approach over anything else. It's like they don't know how to mix audio!\n\nI'm not saying there isn't anything here but for what there is, it's still an extremely shallow experience and I'm appalled that it costs $12 not on sale. I feel like this game has a lot of lost potential to actually do something different and be great but at the end of the day the entire thing falls short.",
